\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A 12 V supply is connected across three resistors in series: $3\,\Omega$, $2\,\Omega$ and $1\,\Omega$. Point B, at the negative terminal, is earthed. Find the potentials at $P$ and $Q$ (after the first and second resistors) when moving from the positive terminal, and verify that each resistor's drop is proportional to its resistance.

\begin{center}
  \begin{circuitikz}[x=1cm, y=1cm, line width=1pt]
    \draw (0,2.5) to[battery1, l=12 V] (0,0);
    \draw (0,2.5) to[R, l=$3\,\Omega$] (2,2.5)
      to[R, l=$2\,\Omega$] (4,2.5)
      to[R, l=$1\,\Omega$] (6,2.5);
    \draw (6,2.5) to[short] (6,0) -- (0,0);
    \node[ground, scale=0.8] at (6,0) {};
    \node[right] at (6.3,0) {$B$};
    \node[above] at (2,2.5) {$P$};
    \node[above] at (4,2.5) {$Q$};
  \end{circuitikz}
  \captionof{figure}{A 12 V supply across three resistors ($3\,\Omega$, $2\,\Omega$, $1\,\Omega$) in series, earthed at B. The larger the resistance, the bigger the share of the 12 V that it drops.}
\end{center}

\textbf{Solution.} Total resistance is $3+2+1=6\,\Omega$, so the current is
\[
I=\frac{12}{6}=2.0\text{ A}.
\]
Earthing sets $V_B=0$ but does not by itself change the currents. Each resistor drops a p.d. $V_k=IR_k$, so the drops are proportional to the resistances:
\[
V_{3\Omega}=2\times 3=\boxed{6\text{ V}},\quad
V_{2\Omega}=2\times 2=\boxed{4\text{ V}},\quad
V_{1\Omega}=2\times 1=\boxed{2\text{ V}}.
\]
These add up to $6+4+2=12$ V. Starting from the positive terminal ($+12$ V), the potential decreases by 6 V across the $3\,\Omega$, then by 4 V across the $2\,\Omega$, then by 2 V across the $1\,\Omega$ to reach earth:
\[
V_P=+6\text{ V},\qquad V_Q=+2\text{ V},\qquad V_B=0\text{ V}.
\]
The potential ladder below lines up with the circuit, and the height of each step is exactly the drop across that resistor.

