\myskip
\textbf{\large Ohm's law: $V=IR$}

So far we have two separate quantities: the \textbf{potential difference} $V$ across a component (energy per unit charge), and the \textbf{current} $I$ through it (charge per unit time). Ohm's law is the single relation that ties them together for an ohmic conductor (one whose $V$--$I$ graph is a straight line through the origin):
\[
\boxed{V=IR}\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad I=\frac{V}{R}\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad R=\frac{V}{I}.
\]
The unit of $R$ is the ohm: $1\,\Omega=1\text{ V A}^{-1}$. The same equation can be read three ways: given any two of $V$, $I$, $R$, the third follows.

\myskip
\textbf{(1) One component at a time.} The law applies to a \emph{single} resistor (or any single ohmic element). It does not directly apply to a whole network of several components — the network's total $R$ is not just one of the individual $R$'s. To use $V=IR$ on a network we first replace it by its \textbf{equivalent resistance} (see below) and only then apply the law.

\begin{center}
  \begin{circuitikz}[x=1cm, y=1cm, >=stealth, line width=1pt]
    % Battery on the left
    \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l=$\mathcal E$] (0,3);
    % Top wire: ammeter in series with the resistor
    \draw (0,3) -- (1,3);
    \draw (1,3) to[ammeter, l=$A$] (2.5,3);
    \draw (2.5,3) -- (3.5,3);
    \draw (3.5,3) to[R, l=$R$] (5.5,3);
    \draw (5.5,3) -- (6,3);
    % Right and bottom wires return
    \draw (6,3) -- (6,0) -- (0,0);
    % Voltmeter across the resistor (drawn below the resistor so it doesn't collide)
    \draw (3.5,2.4) -- (3.5,1.6) -- (5.5,1.6) -- (5.5,2.4);
    \node[draw,fill=white,inner sep=2pt] at (4.5,1.6) {$V$};
    \draw (3.5,2.4) -- (3.5,2.0);
    \draw (5.5,2.4) -- (5.5,2.0);
  \end{circuitikz}
  \captionof{figure}{Reading $V$ and $I$ for a single resistor. The ammeter $A$ is in series (carries the same current as $R$); the voltmeter $V$ is in parallel with $R$ (reads the p.d. across it). Then $V=IR$ for that one component.}
\end{center}

\textbf{How to use it.}
\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=1pt,topsep=2pt]
  \item Isolate one component. Write down \emph{its own} $V$ and \emph{its own} $I$.
  \item Apply $V=IR$ to just that component — never to a mixed bag of components.
  \item Move on to the next component.
\end{enumerate}
This is the single most important habit in circuit problems. It is why the answers in a series string are different from those in a parallel string even when the same components are involved.

\myskip
\textbf{(2) A system of components: the equivalent resistance $R_{\rm eqv}$.} When several resistors are joined together they behave, \emph{as seen from the battery}, like a single resistor. That single notional resistor is the \textbf{equivalent resistance} $R_{\rm eqv}$: the one resistor that draws the same total current $I$ from the same supply $V$ as the whole network does.

\[
\boxed{V=I\,R_{\rm eqv}}\qquad\text{($V$ = supply p.d.,\quad $I$ = total current drawn from the supply).}
\]

The trick is to compute $R_{\rm eqv}$ from the way the components are connected, and then $V=IR_{\rm eqv}$ gives the total current in one step.

\begin{center}
