\textbf{Putting it together.} Whichever way a network is wired, the procedure is the same:
\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=1pt,topsep=2pt]
  \item Collapse the network into a single equivalent resistance $R_{\rm eqv}$ using the series and parallel rules above (re-apply them on sub-groups until only one $R$ is left).
  \item Apply $V=IR_{\rm eqv}$ once to the whole network to get the total current from the supply.
  \item Then, and only then, go back to individual components and apply $V=IR$ to each one separately to find the current through it and the p.d. across it.
\end{enumerate}

\myskip
\textbf{The potential ladder (hill diagram).} Follow one coulomb of charge around a complete loop. The source lifts it to a higher potential (an energy gain per unit charge), and each component it passes through lowers the potential again (an energy loss per unit charge). Since the charge must return to exactly the potential it started at, the total rise must equal the total fall around any closed loop:

